
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <a href="https://plato.stanford.edu/">Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</a> (SEP) gets its own profile in <em>The New Yorker</em> today.</p>



<span id="more-27670"></span>



<p class="has-text-align-right has-rule-color has-text-color has-link-color wp-elements-2 wp-block-paragraph">.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-large is-resized"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1024" height="618" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Stanford-Encyclopedia-of-Philosophy-New-Yorker-1024x618.png" alt="" class="wp-image-27671" style="aspect-ratio:1.656984086890629;width:649px;height:auto" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Stanford-Encyclopedia-of-Philosophy-New-Yorker-1024x618.png 1024w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Stanford-Encyclopedia-of-Philosophy-New-Yorker-300x181.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Stanford-Encyclopedia-of-Philosophy-New-Yorker-768x464.png 768w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Stanford-Encyclopedia-of-Philosophy-New-Yorker-1536x928.png 1536w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Stanford-Encyclopedia-of-Philosophy-New-Yorker-2048x1237.png 2048w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Stanford-Encyclopedia-of-Philosophy-New-Yorker-250x151.png 250w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <a href="https://www.newyorker.com/culture/annals-of-inquiry/the-monumental-achievement-of-the-stanford-encyclopedia-of-philosophy">profile</a>&#8216;s author, <a href="https://sheonhan.net/about/">Sheon Han</a>, writes about how he &#8220;laughed in disbelief&#8221; when, years ago as an undergraduate, he learned of the SEP. Unfortunately, it was <em>after</em> he had just submitted a paper for a class. Reading its entry on the topic of his paper, he says, was eye-opening:</p>



<blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><em>After reading the entry, which ran to more than ten thousand words, I felt at once elated and rebuked: I’d just been exposed to perhaps the finest distillation of what humanity had to say about the topic, but it was also clear that my paper didn’t contain an iota of original insight.</em></p>
</blockquote>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The profile relays some of the history of the SEP and the context of its beginnings:</p>



<blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><em>The S.E.P. began [in 1995] with two text files on Zalta’s computer, about Turing machines and the German philosopher Gottlob Frege. Despite these modest beginnings, its telos was clear: to become a peer-reviewed reference work that would reflect advancements in the field, an objective that its more physical counterparts had failed to achieve. The most comprehensive philosophy encyclopedia at the time, the Macmillan Encyclopedia, from 1967, had yet to include major developments from luminaries such as Saul Kripke, Hilary Putnam, and John Rawls.</em></p>
</blockquote>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The article also discusses the SEP&#8217;s professional significance and a bit about its editorial processes. </p>



